  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 235 PM EDT Thu Oct 29 2026\n\n- Scattered Showers and Isolated Storms along and south I 94 today \n\n The stalled frontal boundary remains draped across southern Lower\nMichigan. Latest mesoanalysis shows 30kts of shear through the\nlowest row of counties in MI. While there is very little SFC based\nCAPE, there is enough instability aloft that coupled with the\nboundary and the shear to allow for storms to develop along the\ncold front. \n These storms are not expected to become severe but given the time\nof year and the height of the -10C, small hail is possible. The\nmain area of concern is along and south of the I 94 corridor.\nExpect scattered showers and isolated storms moving from west to \neast through the afternoon hours before the cold front moves\neastward early this evening. \n \n Temperatures will drop behind the front with Min T's dropping\nbelow freezing tonight along the US 10 corridor. Elsewhere Mix\ntemperatures will be in the mid to upper 30s. \n\n- Decreasing chances for rain for this weekend\n\nModels are becoming in good agreement for the upcoming system.\nHigh pressure at the surface through the mid levels will dominate\nthe pattern through the eastern half of the US. That high will\nstymie the approaching upper level perturbation making its way\nthrough the Great Plains.\n The overall pattern has a negatively tilted ridge that will\nextend through the upper mid west into Canada this weekend, along\nwith an upper level short wave that will move through the Great\nLakes this weekend. \n That perturbation will move through Saturday into Sunday but \nmost mid range guidance has the best moisture bifurcating Southern\nLower Michigan. That said, there remains a chance that scattered \nlight rain showers are possible Saturday, mainly through the \nsouthwest. \n\n The better instability with that upper level trough could arrive\nlate Sunday into Monday. NAEFS ensembles has a broad area of\nslightly anomalous PWATS moving over Lower Michigan late Sunday\ninto Monday. The best moisture is to the southeast, however a\nnegatively tilted mid level shortwave could bring enough\ninstability for showers and perhaps a storm Sunday night into \nMonday morning.\n\n- Change in Pattern with chances for rain next week\n\n The pattern is coming into a bit of focus through the first half\nof next week. As we know, the models do better with high \npressure, and as there is a stronger ridge Monday and tuesday, \nthat is allowing for more consistency in the trends. That said, \nMonday is looking drier, with converging lows moving through the \nregion on Tuesday. The best moisture remain north of southern \nLower but scatter showers are possible early Tuesday from warm air\nadvection out ahead of an approaching system. From then on out \nthe ensembles and resulting guidance diverge on any coherent \nsolution. There remains the potential for a frontal pattern with \nthe chance for showers and storms moving through the Great Lakes,\nWednesday and Thursday. Temperatures will remain above normal \nthrough the period with highs in the mid to upper 60s.",
